# Git Workflow

## Branches
- **main**: Production-ready code only. No direct commits. No force pushes. No deletions.
- **feat/**: Branch from main, merge to main. Format: `feat/descriptive-name`
- **hotfix/**: Urgent production fixes. Format: `hotfix/*`
- Delete feature/hotfix branches after merge

## Commits
Format: `type(scope): description`
- `feat`: New feature
- `fix`: Bug fix  
- `docs`: Documentation
- `refactor`: Code refactoring
- `test`: Adding tests
- `chore`: Maintenance tasks
- Don't break lines in the commit message. You can logically break into list/paragraph breaks if needed.

## Versioning
Semantic versioning: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
- MAJOR: Breaking API changes
- MINOR: Backwards-compatible features
- PATCH: Backwards-compatible fixes

## Pull Requests
- All changes via PRs
- Minimum 1 approval required
- CI checks must pass
- Branch must be up-to-date
- No direct commits to main
## Nothing deployment-specific or personal in a tracked file
This repository publishes to GitHub. Code, tests, docs, comments and throwaway
scripts must not carry a real email, an AWS account id or ARN, an API Gateway or
IoT endpoint, a Cognito pool id, a key or a token — not even a dev-only one.
Read the value from an env var with no baked-in default and fail loudly when it is
unset, or use a placeholder (`example.com`, `<account-id>`, `YOUR_API_BASE`); test
fixtures use `example.com` addresses and the conventional fake account ids.
`.gitleaks.toml` enforces this in CI and in the `make githooks` pre-commit hook. A
genuine false positive goes in that file as a value-scoped allowlist on the rule it
trips, with a comment saying why.
